@page "/db-check"
@using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore
@using TenExCards.Data
@inject IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext> DbFactory

@* Proof that the persistence spine READS AND WRITES from the deployed app. A connectivity check
   would prove neither. This is the persistence counterpart to CircuitCheck.razor and carries the
   same scheduled death: S-01 deletes this page, its nav entry, the SpineProbe entity and its table.

   STATICALLY RENDERED ON PURPOSE — note the absence of an @rendermode line. That is not an
   omission. A Blazor SSR form emits an antiforgery token automatically; an interactive-server
   button would dispatch over the circuit, post no form and mint no token — leaving Phase 3's
   restart-survival check (the only check that regresses if Data Protection key persistence breaks)
   with nothing to run against. Circuit liveness is already proven by /circuit-check, which lives
   until S-01 deletes both. Adding @rendermode here would silently remove the evidence. *@

<PageTitle>Database check</PageTitle>

<h1>Database check</h1>

<p>
    Submitting the form writes one row to the database and re-renders this page from a fresh read.
    If the count increases and survives a restart, the spine reads and writes durably.
</p>

<p role="status">Probe rows: <strong>@rowCount</strong></p>

<p role="status">
    Most recent write (UTC):
    <strong>@(mostRecentUtc?.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss") ?? "none yet")</strong>
</p>

@* Do NOT add <AntiforgeryToken /> here. A statically-rendered EditForm with a FormName already
   emits the hidden __RequestVerificationToken field itself. Adding the component too renders the
   field TWICE, the POST submits two values for one name, and validation fails with "A valid
   antiforgery token was not provided" — which reads as a missing token rather than a duplicated
   one. Verified 2026-09-10 by inspecting the rendered markup. *@
<EditForm Model="this" method="post" OnValidSubmit="WriteProbeAsync" FormName="write-probe">
    <button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">Write a probe row</button>
</EditForm>

@code {
    private int rowCount;
    private DateTime? mostRecentUtc;

    // A context per operation from the factory, rather than one held open across the request.
    // Blazor Server memory is per-circuit and this app deliberately keeps a whole passage plus its
    // candidates there later; a context left open would add to that ceiling for no benefit.
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => await LoadAsync();

    private async Task WriteProbeAsync()
    {
        await using (var db = await DbFactory.CreateDbContextAsync())
        {
            db.SpineProbes.Add(new SpineProbe { WrittenAtUtc = DateTime.UtcNow });
            await db.SaveChangesAsync();
        }

        // Re-read rather than incrementing in memory: the point is to prove the row came back out
        // of the database, not that a counter went up.
        await LoadAsync();
    }

    private async Task LoadAsync()
    {
        await using var db = await DbFactory.CreateDbContextAsync();
        rowCount = await db.SpineProbes.CountAsync();
        mostRecentUtc = await db.SpineProbes
            .OrderByDescending(p => p.WrittenAtUtc)
            .Select(p => (DateTime?)p.WrittenAtUtc)
            .FirstOrDefaultAsync();
    }
}
